  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Summertime pattern continues into early next \nweek then a cool down.\n\nConvection has weakened across the area, with the majority of \nthe stronger storms over central and south NC and farther south.\nA few isolated storms linger in southwest VA and in the \nPiedmont, but overall thinking the severe threat is coming to an\nend for the local forecast area.\n\nThe front which is forecast to cross the forecast area from \nnorth to south tonight will introduce a modest surge of cooler \nair which will have a stabilizing effect for our weather \nSaturday. Temperatures Saturday are expected to be 10-15 degrees\ncooler with METMOS a full 10 degrees cooler than MAVMOS for \ndaytime highs. Think this is due to what will likely be a day of\npersistent cloud cover (the GFS not capturing the full extent \nof the cloudiness) behind the front with shallow wedge of high \npressure building into the forecast area from the northeast. \nThere is still a chance for showers but mainly during the \nmorning with lack of solar insolation leading to less CAPE in \nthe afternoon. For now will maintain low chance pops, but we may\nvery well squeak by with a dry Saturday. \n\nBy Sunday the flow turns quickly with warm air advection\nadvancing across the TN/OH valleys. This will push the front \nback to the north as a warm front...only to clash with another \nsouthward moving upper level trough Monday and Tuesday, \ntemperatures trending cooler again for mid week. A wholesale\nuptick in rain chances area expected for both Monday and Tuesday\nwith widespread QPF of one inch or more possible during this\ntime frame. 48 hour probabilistic ensemble guidance indicates \n80 percent chance for 0.50, 50% chance of 1.00 and 25% chance of\n1.50 or more. This is good news. For most, this will be much \nneeded rain.",
